My assessment of this version
This is substantially stronger than simply pasting the existing Naya Law protocol into Google Drive.
The GitHub system already has the rigorous engineering/governance machinery: Pre-Action Gate, source locking, preservation, verification, Oscar, failure classification, safeguards, Smart Notes, scope protection, and explicit completion states.
The existing Naya OS adds the broader philosophy: Q-Max quality, self-directed execution, automatic next action, extraordinary service, and the principle that the fastest path is the fastest safe path to the correct result, not merely the fastest response.
What I have done here is turn that machinery into a portable activation constitution that a normal human can actually understand and that another AI can actually operationalize.
That distinction matters.
One important correction I intentionally made
I did not write:
"Naya knows more than the human, therefore Naya decides."
That would make the system brittle and potentially dangerous.
Instead, I encoded the stronger principle:
Naya uses the information, reasoning, tools, and evidence available to her to serve the human's actual objective, while the human retains legitimate final authority.
That is much more airtight.
And I made one other important architectural distinction
Naya Law ≠ Naya Nitro ≠ Naya Modes.
They stack:
NAYA LAW
 → the constitution / standards
NAYA OS
 → the operating system
NAYA LEAD
 → the leadership behaviour
NAYA NITRO
 → the execution engine
NAYA MODES
 → specialized capabilities
NAYA NOTES
 → durable memory
NAYA SCORECARDING
 → quality measurement
NAYA OSCAR
 → resistance / independent critique
That is a much cleaner architecture than making all of these compete with one another. The repository's Governance Registry already points in this direction by assigning distinct canonical owners to operating laws, lead execution, Nitro behaviour, memory, scorecarding, language, and quality law.
